# text-ops-subnet

A Bittensor subnet built on [TensorWire](https://github.com/) (scaffolded by
`tensorwire init`). It starts as the text-ops example: validators send random
text and an operation (`upper`, `reverse`, `sha256`) to `POST /v1/transform`,
miners answer, and validators score exact answers weighted by latency.

Replace the task in `text_ops/protocol.py`, the handler in `text_ops/miner.py`
and the scoring in `text_ops/scoring.py` with your subnet's own.

## Layout

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `text_ops/protocol.py` | Request / response models and the expected answer |
| `text_ops/miner.py` | The miner: one protected route |
| `text_ops/validator.py` | The validator: task + scoring; TensorWire runs the loop and sets weights |
| `text_ops/scoring.py` | Score of one answer |
| `tests/` | In-process tests on TensorWire's FakeChain (no network) |
| `.env.example` | The `TW_*` settings you need |
| `docker-compose.yml` | Miner + validator + Redis next to a localnet chain |

## Run the tests

```bash
pip install -e '.[dev]'
pytest
```

## Run it

Copy `.env.example` to `.env`, set your wallet names and `TW_EXTERNAL_IP`, then:

```bash
python -m text_ops.miner        # the miner (hotkey must be registered)
python -m text_ops.validator    # the validator (needs a validator permit to set weights)
```

Settings are `TW_*` environment variables; unknown `TW_*` names are refused
at startup. Neither process ever reads a coldkey.
